# MAIL(1)

## NAME

mail - send or receive mail among users

## SYNOPSIS

| **mail** person ...
| **mail** [ **-r** ] [ **-q** ] [ **-p** ] [ **-f** file ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Mail* with no argument prints a user's mail, message-by-message, in last-in, first-out order;
the optional argument **-r** causes first-in, first-out order. If the **-p** flag is given, the
mail is printed with no questions asked; otherwise, for each message, *mail* reads a line from
the standard input to direct disposition of the message.

newline
: Go on to next message.

d
: Delete message and go on to the next.

p
: Print message again.

-
: Go back to previous message.

s [ *file* ] ...
: Save the message in the named *files* (`mbox` default).

w [ *file* ] ...
: Save the message, without a header, in the named *files* (`mbox` default).

m [ *person* ] ...
: Mail the message to the named *persons*. Note: v7 documented yourself as the default, but a
bare `m` has never done that in this code — it takes the no-argument arm and mails nothing at
all. The behaviour is v7's and is left alone; the sentence is what was wrong.

EOT (control-D)
: Put unexamined mail back in the mailbox and stop.

q
: Same as EOT.

x
: Exit, without changing the mailbox file.

!command
: Escape to the Shell to do command.

?
: Print a command summary.

An interrupt stops the printing of the current letter. The optional argument **-q** causes
*mail* to exit after interrupts without changing the mailbox.

When *persons* are named, *mail* takes the standard input up to an end-of-file (or a line with
just `.`) and adds it to each *person's* `mail` file. The message is preceded by the sender's
name and a postmark. Lines that look like postmarks are prepended with `>`. A *person* is
usually a user name recognized by login(1).

Note: **there is no remote mail here.** v7 let a *person* be `system!user` and handed the letter
to uux(1); this system has neither uucp(1) nor uux(1), both refused in the port, and the
`<whoami.h>` that named the local system was pruned from the header tree. A *person* containing
an exclamation mark is refused with

| mail: no remote mail: *person*

the letter is saved in `dead.letter` as any other undeliverable one is, and *mail* exits 1.

The **-f** option causes the named file, e.g. `mbox`, to be printed as if it were the mail
file.

Note: the file name is bounded at 128 characters and a longer one is refused rather than
truncated — a shortened path names some other file, and *mail* is willing to rewrite what it is
given. Note: **-f does not lock**. The lock below is a protocol among the processes that share
`/usr/spool/mail`; a private `mbox` is not one of those, and locking it would change the mode of
a file of your own for no reader's benefit.

Each user owns his own mailbox, which is by default generally readable but not writable — mode
0644, owned by the recipient, in a `/usr/spool/mail` that is mode 0755 and the super-user's, so
that nobody can unlink anybody else's mailbox. The command does not delete an empty mailbox nor
change its mode, so a user may make it unreadable if desired.

Note: `/bin/mail` is **setuid to the super-user**, because creating a mailbox for someone else
needs chown(2) and taking the lock needs chmod(2) on a file *mail* does not own. It gives that
privilege back — real user-id and all — as the very first act of printing mail, so the `!`
escape, the `s` command, **-f** and everything else typed at the `?` prompt run as whoever is
typing. That is also why `m` hands the letter to a fresh *mail* rather than writing the mailbox
itself: by then it no longer can.

Note: at most 300 letters are read from one mailbox. Past that *mail* refuses with a diagnostic;
v7 had the same limit and stored past the end of its own table instead.

Note: there is no `rmail`. v7 linked one to *mail* so that uux(1) could deliver a letter without
offering the interactive half; with no uux(1) there is nothing to link it for.

When a user logs in he is informed of the presence of mail. Note: this became true only when
*mail* was ported — login(1) has probed for the mailbox since it was written, and until then
never found one.

## FILES

| /usr/spool/mail/\* mailboxes
| /etc/passwd to identify sender and locate persons
| mbox saved mail
| /tmp/ma\* temp file
| dead.letter unmailable text

## SEE ALSO

write(1), login(1), passwd(5)

Note: v7 also named xsend(1) and uucp(1) here. Neither is on this system.

## BUGS

There is a locking mechanism intended to prevent two senders from accessing the same mailbox,
but it is not perfect and races are possible.

Note: the lock is the mailbox's **owner-execute bit**, set while a letter is being added and
cleared afterwards, and a lock more than sixty seconds old is treated as stale and taken. The
race v7 admits to is that stat(2) and chmod(2) are two calls with nothing between them holding
the file: two senders can both find the bit clear. There is no flock(2) or fcntl(2) on this
system to do better with.
